trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Both the ECG interpretation and mediastinal width remain unreported after multiple targeted requests. With 10/16 turns used and critical discriminatory data still missing, I must shift to a test that *directly visualizes cardiac structure and function* and can simultaneously assess for myocardial contusion, wall motion abnormalities, pericardial effusion, and valvular/aortic root injury — all in one modality. Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) is ideal: it is sensitive for regional wall motion abnormalities (suggesting contusion), detects even small pericardial effusions (ruling out tamponade), evaluates aortic root integrity, and assesses left ventricular systolic function. It is non-invasive, widely available in acute settings, and provides real-time functional data that ECG and troponin cannot.

[TEST] Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) — report should include:  
- Left ventricular wall motion (hypokinesis, akinesis);  
- Presence/size of pericardial effusion;  
- Aortic root diameter and integrity;  
- Left ventricular ejection fraction.

